===Spain vs Russia===
[[File:ESP-RUS (4).jpg|thumb|[[Marco Asensio]] (left) and [[Roman Zobnin]] (right) challenge for possession of the ball.]]
The teams had met in six previous games, most recently in a friendly in 2017, which ended in a 3–3 draw. Playing as the [[Soviet Union national football team|Soviet Union]], the teams had faced each other five times.<ref name="statistical kit"/> Russia only had one victory over Spain.

In the 12th minute, [[Marco Asensio]]'s free-kick from deep on the right saw [[Sergei Ignashevich]] grappling with [[Sergio Ramos]] at the back post, the ball bouncing past [[Igor Akinfeev]] off the defender's heel. Russia were then awarded a penalty when the ball bounced off [[Artem Dzyuba]]’s head onto [[Gerard Piqué]]’s arm in the box after a corner from the right. [[Artem Dzyuba]] shot home to the right of the net from 12 yards to send the sides into half-time level. In the 85th minute, Akinfeev got down low to his right to save a shot from [[Andrés Iniesta]], the goalkeeper then denied [[Iago Aspas]] on the rebound. Piqué and Ramos both appeared to be held from a set-piece but, after a VAR check, the referee waved away Spain's appeals. In the penalty shoot-out, Akinfeev kept out a [[Koke (footballer, born 1992)|Koke]] effort and saw Aspas' effort diverted away by his leg to give Russia the win; as all Russia's penalty kicks ended up successful.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Smith |first1=Jamie |title=Akinfeev the shoot-out hero in huge World Cup shock |url=http://www.goal.com/en-za/match/spain-v-russia/676bo6djvlsm6uduf8evk7jzd |publisher=Goal.com |date=1 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180702011131/http://www.goal.com/en-za/match/spain-v-russia/676bo6djvlsm6uduf8evk7jzd |archive-date=2 July 2018}}</ref>

This result meant Spain lost three of their four World Cup penalty shoot-outs (after losing to [[1986 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Spain vs Belgium|Belgium in 1986]], beating the [[2002 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Spain vs Republic of Ireland|Republic of Ireland in 2002]] and losing to [[2002 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Spain vs South Korea|South Korea in 2002]]), and had still never defeated a host nation at the FIFA World Cup, after losing to Italy [[1934 FIFA World Cup final tournament#Italy vs Spain|0–1]] in 1934, Brazil [[1950 FIFA World Cup final round#Brazil vs Spain|1–6]] in 1950, and South Korea [[2002 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Spain vs South Korea|after penalties]] in 2002.<ref name="ESP-RUS recap">{{cite web |url=https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/heroic-hosts-shock-spain-in-shootout |title=Heroic hosts shock Spain in shootout |publisher=FIFA |date=1 July 2018 |access-date=1 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520053546/https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/heroic-hosts-shock-spain-in-shootout |archive-date=20 May 2026}}</ref> Russia reached the quarter-final of the World Cup for the first time since the [[Dissolution of the Soviet Union|break-up of the Soviet Union]]. Also was the first win of Russia in a knockout stage of World Cup since [[1966 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Soviet Union vs Hungary|1966]], still like [[Soviet Union national football team|Soviet Union]]. Ignashevich became the oldest player to score an [[List of FIFA World Cup own goals|own goal at the World Cup]], aged 38 years and 352 days.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bevan |first1=Chris |title=Spain 1 Russia 1 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44591345 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=1 July 2018 }}</ref> The match marked the first time in the history of the FIFA World Cup in which a fourth substitution was made during extra time, after [[Aleksandr Yerokhin]] of Russia entered the pitch in the 97th minute.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.eurosport.com/football/russia-s-aleksandr-yerokhin-makes-history-becomes-first-fourth-sub-at-world-cup_sto6828911/story.shtml |title=Russia's Aleksandr Yerokhin makes history, becomes first 'fourth sub' at World Cup |publisher=[[Eurosport]] |date=1 July 2018 |access-date=4 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704092853/https://www.eurosport.com/football/russia-s-aleksandr-yerokhin-makes-history-becomes-first-fourth-sub-at-world-cup_sto6828911/story.shtml |archive-date=4 July 2018}}</ref>

