===Brazil vs Mexico===
The teams had met in 40 previous matches including two games at [[CONCACAF Gold Cup]] finals ([[1996 CONCACAF Gold Cup|1996]] and [[2003 CONCACAF Gold Cup|2003]], both won by Mexico), the [[1999 FIFA Confederations Cup Final]] (won 4–3 by Mexico), and four times in the FIFA World Cup group stage, three won by Brazil and one ending in a draw ([[1950 FIFA World Cup Group 1#Brazil vs Mexico|4–0]] in 1950, [[1954 FIFA World Cup Group 1#Brazil vs Mexico|5–0]] in 1954, [[1962 FIFA World Cup Group 3#Brazil vs Mexico|2–0]] in 1962 and [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group A#Brazil vs Mexico|0–0]] in 2014).<ref name="statistical kit"/>

[[Hirving Lozano]]'s half-volley was well blocked by [[Miranda (footballer, born 1984)|Miranda]], while at the other end, [[Guillermo Ochoa]] saved [[Neymar]]'s drive from just outside the penalty area. After 25 minutes, Neymar raced past [[Edson Álvarez]] in the area and forced Ochoa into a save with his left hand. [[Gabriel Jesus]] went close in the 33rd minute, finding space in a crowded area and drilling in a left-footed strike that Ochoa palmed away. In the 51st minute, Neymar's back-heel on the edge of the area teed up [[Willian (footballer, born 1988)|Willian]] for a burst into the box and his scuffed cross from the left was slid into an empty net by Neymar from close range. With two minutes remaining, Neymar powered through on the left and his low effort was diverted by Ochoa's foot into the path of [[Roberto Firmino]], who tapped the ball into an empty net from close range.<ref name="BRA-MEX recap">{{cite web |url=https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/neymar-leads-brazil-to-last-eight |title=Neymar leads Brazil to the last eight |publisher=FIFA |date=2 July 2018 |access-date=2 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520053829/https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/neymar-leads-brazil-to-last-eight |archive-date=20 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Atkinson |first1=Guy |title=Neymar and Firmino book Selecao's quarter-final spot |url=http://www.goal.com/en-in/match/brazil-v-mexico/67m6apzl9xpgeh0ronhe2aqy1 |publisher=Goal.com |date=2 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180710090326/http://www.goal.com/en-in/match/brazil-v-mexico/67m6apzl9xpgeh0ronhe2aqy1 |archive-date=10 July 2018}}</ref>

Since the introduction of the round of 16 in 1986, Mexico were eliminated at this stage of the World Cup seven times – more than twice as many as any other nation. [[2022 FIFA World Cup|4 years later]] they were eliminated from the group stages for the first time since [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jennings |first1=Patrick |title=Brazil 2 Mexico 0 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44597762 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=2 July 2018 }}</ref> They were eliminated at this stage once again on home soil in [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026.]] This was also Mexico's fourth defeat at the hands of Brazil. In addition, with Neymar's and Firmino's goals, Brazil have now finally surpassed the record of all-time World Cup goals by any team with 228 goals (after being tied to Germany with 226 goals prior to this match) after constantly tying up the record with Germany in the group stage of this World Cup.

