===Sweden vs Switzerland===
[[File:SWE-SWI (15).jpg|thumb|[[Marcus Berg]] (left) attempts to take possession of the ball away from [[Johan Djourou]] (right).]]
The teams had faced each other in 28 previous matches, which included three matches in [[1962 FIFA World Cup qualification (UEFA – Group 1)|1962 FIFA World Cup qualification]], Switzerland winning twice (3–2 and 2–1) and Sweden winning once (4–0), and also twice in [[1978 FIFA World Cup qualification (UEFA)|1978 FIFA World Cup qualification]], both matches ending in a 2–1 Sweden win.<ref name="statistical kit"/>

[[Stephan Lichtsteiner]] and [[Fabian Schär]] were both ruled out through suspension. [[Albin Ekdal]]'s volley missed the target prior to the break from a [[Mikael Lustig]] cross. [[Ola Toivonen]] fired over when well positioned inside the penalty area before [[Emil Forsberg]] finally broke the deadlock, his right foot shot from just outside the penalty area clipping [[Manuel Akanji]] to beat [[Yann Sommer]]. Switzerland sent on forwards [[Breel Embolo]] and [[Haris Seferovic]], and the latter tested Sweden goalkeeper [[Robin Olsen]] with a header in the closing stages. Sweden were denied the chance to double their lead from the spot following [[Michael Lang (footballer, born 1991)|Michael Lang]]'s late dismissal for taking out [[Martin Olsson]], with Sommer beating away Toivonen's driven free-kick after a lengthy delay.<ref name="SWE-SUI recap">{{cite web |url=https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/headline-swedes-squeeze-into-the-last-eight |title=Swedes squeeze into the last eight |publisher=FIFA |date=3 July 2018 |access-date=3 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520054145/https://inside.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2018russia/news/headline-swedes-squeeze-into-the-last-eight |archive-date=20 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Lancaster |first1=Rob |title=Swedes dream thanks to Forsberg's moment of fortune |url=http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/sweden-v-switzerland/report/687o9dkklut4zf00dqwcr95ux |publisher=Goal.com |date=3 July 2018 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180703220454/http://www.goal.com/en-us/match/sweden-v-switzerland/report/687o9dkklut4zf00dqwcr95ux |archive-date=3 July 2018}}</ref>

Sweden qualified for their first World Cup quarter-final since 1994. Sweden won back-to-back World Cup matches for the first time since 1958, when they won the quarter-final and semi-final on their way to the [[1958 FIFA World Cup Final|final as host nation that year]]. Olsen kept his third clean sheet of the 2018 World Cup, a joint-record for a Swedish goalkeeper in World Cup finals history (also three clean sheets for [[Ronnie Hellström]] in 1974 and [[Kalle Svensson]] in 1958).<ref>{{cite web |last1=Fletcher |first1=Paul |title=Sweden 1 Switzerland 0 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/44610243 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=3 July 2018 }}</ref>

